is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Campus 2 is healthy at this time; both gateway interfaces are up and reachable. From multiple 10.5.0.x hosts (pc1, pc2, dc_pc5) we can ping 10.5.0.254 with 0% loss, and from multiple 10.6.0.x hosts (pc3, pc4) we can ping 10.6.0.254 with 0% loss. ARP on the clients resolves both gateways to MAC 56:17:5d:ad:4b:94. On the big switch (sshswitch), the gateway interfaces are present and UP: br_v5 has IP 10.5.0.254/24 and br_v6 has IP 10.6.0.254/24; their member VLAN subinterfaces (bond0.5/eth0.5 and bond0.6/eth0.6) are UP and bridged. The access switch feeding Campus 2 (switch4) shows correct L2 bridging: VLAN 5 ports (eth1/2/3/6) and VLAN 6 ports (eth4/5) are bridged, with trunk subinterfaces eth0.5 and eth0.6 attached to the respective VLAN bridges. This explains why clients can ARP and reach the gateway “thingies” successfully now.

Localization: Gateways reside on sshswitch (bridges br_v5 and br_v6). The L2 uplink path is switch4 eth0 ↔ sshswitch eth0, with VLAN 5/6 subinterfaces carrying the traffic.

Root cause: No current fault detected; the reported “gateways off after reload” condition cannot be reproduced.

Corrective fix: No changes required. If the problem was intermittent after a reboot, verify that the VLAN subinterfaces and bridge interfaces on sshswitch are configured to auto-start on boot and that the uplink trunk comes up promptly; otherwise, the environment is operating normally now. 
=== END ===